    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green. The full test suite consists of `AcceptanceTest` (the only `@Test`-bearing class), which now passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required that a created owner's response carry `bulkSignupWarning: true` when more than 80 owners had already been created for that day, else `false`. Since the value is asserted via `GET /api/owners/{id}` after creation, it's a snapshot taken at creation time \u2014 exactly like the existing `namesakeCount`. I followed that established pattern:\n\n- **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`): added a persisted `bulkSignupWarning` boolean field with getter/setter, mapped to a new `bulk_signup_warning` column.\n- **Schema** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres `schema.sql`): added the `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n- **Service** (`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`): added `isBulkSignupWarranted(LocalDate)`, which reuses the existing `ownerRepository.countByRegistrationDate(...)` accumulation path (the same one the daily-limit rule uses) against a new `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 80`, returning `count > 80`.\n- **Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`): sets the warning on the owner right before saving, next to the `namesakeCount` snapshot.\n- **OpenAPI** (`openapi.yml`): added the read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` schema; MapStruct auto-wired the mapping in both directions (verified in the generated `OwnerMapperImpl`).\n\nEach concern stays in its own place \u2014 threshold rule in the service, persistence on the entity, presentation via the mapper \u2014 with no duplication and no method turning into a catch-all.",
